messages:
aj: quick check — packaging an auth provider for the TS SDK to run on Next Edge (Vercel) and Vite SSR. importing our CJS-style `auth-providers/cookie-provider` throws at runtime: "SyntaxError: The requested module '/node_modules/auth-providers/cookie-provider/index.js' does not provide an export named 'default'". anyone seen this with ESM-only edge runtimes?

maria: yep, seen that. Edge runtimes (and native ESM builds) expect ESM exports. If the package.json has "type": "commonjs" or ships cjs, you hit that. What does the provider package export look like?

aj: it's a simple module that ends with `module.exports = { getAuth: ..., setAuth: ... }`. no `exports.default`. consumers import like `import provider from '@our/auth-providers/cookie-provider'`. it works in Node CJS and in local dev where bundler shims, but breaks in the Edge deployment.

tyler: quick workaround: use a tiny ESM wrapper that does `import cjs from './cookie-provider.cjs'; export const default = cjs; export const getAuth = cjs.getAuth;` — basically re-export. Vite/Next will happily bundle that.

sophie: also consider changing the published package to provide both entrypoints: `exports.import` (ESM) and `exports.require` or a separate `module` field. That keeps consumers working without wrapper hacks.

aj: thanks. is there an approach that avoids changing the package? I'm trying to ship a customer fix quickly.

maria: if you can't change the package, dynamic `const provider = await import('.../cookie-provider').then(m=>m.default||m)` usually handles both ESM/CJS at runtime in node, but not always in edge runtime where `import()` may error if package is CJS only. For bundlers, rewriting import to `const provider = await import('.../cookie-provider').then(m=>m.default||m)` often gets transformed.

tyler: for Vite specifically, you can add an alias in `vite.config.ts` to point the import to your wrapper file during build:

```ts
// vite.config.ts
export default defineConfig({
  resolve: {
    alias: {
      '@our/auth-providers/cookie-provider': '/src/wrappers/cookie-provider-esm.js'
    }
  }
})
```

that avoids runtime surprises.

sophie: FYI if the issue is `process.env` or other node globals missing in edge — often bundlers replace those at build time. For auth tokens in edge, prefer reading from request headers/cookies instead of process.env at runtime.

aj: related weirdness — streaming responses from SDK with auth hooking: when I wrap the fetch to inject Authorization header in edge, streaming stalls (readable stream backpressure?). anyone debugged streaming+auth wrap in Next Edge?

maria: yep. If you override `fetch` to do `response.body.pipeThrough(...)` make sure you do not read the whole body before returning. Edge expects a real ReadableStream. Also respect `AbortSignal` and forward it: `fetch(url, { signal })`.

tyler: small snippet that worked for me:

```js
export async function fetchWithAuth(input, init = {}) {
  const token = await getEdgeToken();
  init.headers = { ...(init.headers||{}), Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` };
  return fetch(input, init); // return the raw fetch response (do not await .text())
}
```

If you need transforms, use `response.body.pipeThrough(new TransformStream(...))` but return a `new Response(transformedStream, { headers: response.headers })`.

sophie: also watch for retry logic — if retries re-construct the body or re-read the stream, it will break because streams are one-shot. Use request cloning or buffer small bodies for retries.

aj: good call. our retry wrapper was buffering then attempting to re-use the original stream for subsequent tries. I'll change it to buffer only for <1KB metadata, otherwise fail fast.

devx-bot: FYI: created ticket RDW-PLAT-1123 to track converting cookie-provider to dual ESM/CJS entrypoints. Link: https://redwood.example/PLAT-1123

aj: perfect, thanks all. i'll try the wrapper alias for a hotfix and push a PR to update retry behavior. will ping when I have a repro branch. :thumbsup:
